# WHAT EACH DOCUMENT SAYS ABOUT COUPLINGS, AND WHETHER IT SAYS ANYTHING AT ALL. One row per
# filing in the chosen scope: how many couplings it states, and what its coupling SEARCH
# reported if it states none.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE TWO COLUMNS ARE TWO DIFFERENT FACTS AND AN EMPTY COUPLING TABLE CARRIES ONLY THE
#    FIRST. A filing with no coupling rows is a stack of independent layers OR a document
#    where nobody looked, and which one it is has never been in the coupling table. It is in
#    the search beside it, which is why that element had to exist.
#
# ⭐⭐ THE SCOPE IS A SLOT BECAUSE THE TWO CALLERS GENUINELY DISAGREE, AND BOTH ARE RIGHT.
#    `rules.sql` asks this of the CORPUS: "has anybody tested the independence assumption" is
#    a claim about the evidence, and a fixture that asserts independence on purpose would
#    answer it with a yes nobody earned. `matrices.sql` asks it of EVERY filing, because it is
#    a tour of what the matrix cannot express and the stipulations demonstrate that best.
#    ⛔ This relation was written twice, once in each of those files, with identical joins and
#      different scopes. That is how two copies of one relation start.
-- pm:Stack/pm:couplings against pm:Couplings/pm:absent, over a scope the caller names.
SELECT f.filing,
       count(c.filing) AS couplings_filed,
       s.answer        AS search_answer
FROM      (
    :compose(@scope)
) f
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(entries/couplings.sqlc)
) c USING (filing)
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(epistemics/coupling_searches.sqlc)
) s ON s.filing = f.filing
GROUP BY f.filing, s.answer
